From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 06:46:20 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Add evictable glyphs to the sprite atlas

The sprite atlas keeps every glyph it ever rasterized, keyed by font, size
and subpixel variant. That suits a UI with a handful of text sizes, but text
on a zoomable canvas is drawn at continuously changing sizes, so every zoom
adds glyph rasters and the atlas grows without bound over a session.

Window::with_evictable_glyphs(f) makes the glyphs and emoji painted inside f
evictable: the atlas (AtlasState, so every backend's) records the frame they
were last asked for, and PlatformAtlas::end_frame, which Window::draw calls
once a frame, drops those not used in the last 120 frames once evictable
tiles take more than 16 M texels, least recently used first, down to three
quarters of that. A dropped glyph is rasterized again if it is painted
again. Tiles also asked for outside such a scope are never dropped, and the
text system forgets the raster bounds of dropped glyphs so that cache stays
bounded too. Only tiles unused for 120 frames go, so frames still on the
GPU never read a reused tile.

PlatformAtlas gains get_or_insert_evictable_with and end_frame with default
implementations (atlases that never drop tiles), implemented for the Metal,
wgpu, DirectX (not compiled here) and headless atlases.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
